    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Alfonso I of Aragon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinero_chaqu%C3%A9s_cara_Alifonso_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Willtron, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> was born of a dynastic marriage. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I the Battler</a> of Aragon, conqueror of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1118, died childless in 1134, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-aragon">Ramiro II</a> left the monastery to rule and secure an heir. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila</a> was betrothed in 1137, when still an infant, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona</a>, who governed Aragon as its prince while keeping his own counties. Their son, born Ramon and reigning as Alfonso II, succeeded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1162 and to Aragon on his mother’s abdication in 1164. The union joined an inland kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley with the Catalan counties and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> ports, while each kept its own laws and assemblies. Alfonso added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> in 1172, and the dynasty held Provence and lordships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>. Peter II had himself crowned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1204, fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212 and was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a> in 1213, ending the Crown’s ambitions north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I</a> the Conqueror turned south and seaward, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> from 1229 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, whose capital surrendered in 1238. He created the separate Kingdom of Majorca and the Kingdom of Valencia, each with its own law, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-almizra">Treaty of Almizra</a> of 1244 fixed the boundary of conquest with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/barcelona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barcelona.jpg" alt="Barcelona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_light_over_Barcelona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">M McBey, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The realms of the Crown were very different. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> was a land of dry uplands and irrigated valleys, dominated by great nobles, with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> Muslim peasants; Catalonia combined a seigneurial countryside with the trading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> was a fertile coastal plain where a Christian settler minority ruled a large Muslim population for centuries after the conquest; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> was a busy port between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Commerce bound them together. Catalan, Valencian and Majorcan merchants traded cloth, grain, salt, coral, saffron and slaves across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, held consulates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and followed the maritime customs of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea">Consolat de Mar</a></em>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> supplied grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, and Majorcan cartographers produced works such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a> of 1375. Jewish communities flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, Barcelona, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> and Valencia until the pogroms of 1391 and the expulsion of 1492. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 hit Catalonia especially hard, and in the fifteenth century Valencia overtook Barcelona as the largest and richest city of the Crown, building its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> Exchange, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llotja-de-la-seda">Llotja de la Seda</a>, in 1482–1498. The expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> in 1609–1614 removed perhaps a third of Valencia’s population and a large part of Aragon’s rural labour force.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola.jpg" alt="Battle of Cerignola" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola">Battle of Cerignola</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elgrancapitantrasbatalladeceri%C3%B1ola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Federico de Madrazo y Kuntz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Crown’s armies combined royal and noble cavalry, town militias and the almogavers, light infantry from the frontier who became famous in the wars of the late thirteenth century. Its real strength was at sea. Catalan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drassanes-reials-de-barcelona">Drassanes of Barcelona</a> and commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> won a series of victories against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1282: off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> in 1283, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a> in 1284, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles of Salerno</a> was captured, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-les-formigues">Les Formigues</a> in 1285, which wrecked the French invasion known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aragonese-crusade">Aragonese Crusade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-aragon">James II</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1323, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Iglesias</a> in 1324 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> in 1326, but the island resisted under the judges of Arborea for most of a century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV</a> fought the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-two-peters">War of the Two Peters</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1356 to 1369, in which Aragon suffered repeated invasions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-v-of-leon">Alfonso V</a> the Magnanimous spent years conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, survived capture by the Genoese after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ponza">Battle of Ponza</a> in 1435 and finally entered the city in 1442. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a> the Crown joined Castile in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, completed in 1492, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> won Naples back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cerignola">Cerignola</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a> in 1503. The Crown’s last war ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa">Battle of Almansa</a> in 1707 and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1714.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona.jpg" alt="Usatges of Barcelona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona">Usatges of Barcelona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usatici-Barchinone-usalia-Cathalonie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alfons II d'Aragó, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Crown was a federation of realms under one king, each keeping its own law, parliament, currency and officials: the <em>fueros</em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona">Usatges</a></em> and constitutions of Catalonia, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a></em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I</a> in 1261, and the franchises of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>. Kings swore to respect these laws at their accession, and taxes required the consent of the separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-aragon">Corts of Aragon</a>, Catalonia and Valencia, which sometimes met together at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon-de-campos">Monzón</a>. In Aragon the nobles’ league, the Unión, forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III</a> to grant the General Privilege of 1283 and Alfonso III the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilege-of-the-union">Privilege of the Union</a> in 1287, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV</a> crushed it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-epila">Battle of Épila</a> in 1348. Thereafter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia of Aragon</a>, a judge who could protect subjects against royal officials, became the guardian of Aragonese liberties. In 1319 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-aragon">James II</a> declared the realms inseparable, and the royal archive he founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1318 became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archive-of-the-crown-of-aragon">Archive of the Crown of Aragon</a>. There was no single capital: kings were crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, often resided in Barcelona and Valencia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-v-of-leon">Alfonso V</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Distant realms were governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a>, a system later adopted across the Spanish Monarchy. After the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-aragon">Council of Aragon</a>, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a> in 1494, advised the absent king on the affairs of his eastern realms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/palma-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palma-cathedral.jpg" alt="Palma Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/palma-cathedral">Palma Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathedrale_von_Palma_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> built the first great Iberian empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Through it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1282, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> from 1323 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from 1442 came under Iberian rule, which lasted, through the Spanish Monarchy, until the early eighteenth century and shaped the law, administration and aristocracy of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a>, councils and consulates provided models for the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>. The conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I</a> spread the Catalan language and settlers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, and the Catalan dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghero">Alghero</a> in Sardinia survives today. The Crown’s literature and learning were rich: James I left the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llibre-dels-fets">Llibre dels fets</a></em>, the chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernat-desclot">Bernat Desclot</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-muntaner">Ramon Muntaner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV</a> followed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-llull">Ramon Llull</a> wrote widely in Catalan, Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and Valencia produced the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausias-march">Ausiàs March</a> and the novel <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirant-lo-blanc">Tirant lo Blanc</a></em>, printed in 1490. Gothic buildings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma-cathedral">Palma Cathedral</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llotja-de-la-seda">Valencia Silk Exchange</a> stand as monuments to its trade. Two popes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-callixtus-iii">Callixtus III</a> and Alexander VI of the Borgia family, came from the Kingdom of Valencia. Its tradition of contractual government and regional law left a lasting mark on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where Aragon, Catalonia, Valencia and the Balearic Islands are separate autonomous communities that share the old royal banner of four red bars on gold.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa.jpg" alt="Battle of Almansa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa">Battle of Almansa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balaca-Battle_of_Almansa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Crown’s independent history ended in stages. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-aragon">Martin the Humane</a> without a legitimate heir in 1410, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> in 1412 gave the throne to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand of Antequera</a> of the Castilian Trastámara dynasty. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-v-of-leon">Alfonso V</a>’s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> drew the monarchy’s focus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-aragon">John II</a> faced the Catalan civil war of 1462–1472. The marriage of John’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella of Castile</a> in 1469 led to a dynastic union with the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 1479, and from 1516 both crowns passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The eastern realms kept their laws and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> but became secondary to Castile, whose population and American wealth dominated the Spanish Monarchy. Tensions surfaced in 1591, when Philip II sent an army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> over the affair of his fugitive secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-perez">Antonio Pérez</a> and executed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-lanuza-y-urrea">Juan de Lanuza</a>, and in the Catalan revolt of 1640–1652. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> most of the Crown backed the Habsburg claimant, Archduke Charles. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa">Battle of Almansa</a>, Philip V abolished the laws of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a> of 1707, though Aragonese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> was partly restored in 1711. The Italian realms were lost at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Peace of Utrecht</a> in 1713, and decrees for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1715 and Catalonia in 1716 completed the Crown’s abolition.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> united Aragon, Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> as a federation of self-governing realms under one king, built a maritime empire reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and brought its traditions of contractual government and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> rule into the Spanish Monarchy.</em></p>

